\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Synthesis.}
Our experiments reveal a coherent picture: image tokens remain instruction-blind across all 32 layers, while the readout token is the sole multimodal convergence point.
VLA fine-tuning shifts instruction routing from mid-depth (L9--L16) to an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1 and L31), enabling immediate instruction access ($99.6\%$ vs.\ pretrained $80.2\%$); untrained instruction attention remains below $12\%$.
Training effects concentrate at the readout token ($R^2$ $+0.069$) rather than image representations ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$), with the LLM transforming vision encoder geometry non-monotonically (L8--L13 most action-relevant).

\paragraph{Mechanism-behavior connection.}
The instruction-blindness of image tokens provides a mechanistic explanation for the visual dominance reported behaviorally by Grant et al.~\cite{grant2026}: if image representations carry no instruction-specific information ($|\Delta| < 0.006$), the action pathway cannot distinguish tasks through visual features alone, forcing reliance on the readout token for task conditioning.
This also clarifies a dissociation between geometric and functional alignment: DINOv2 achieves the highest RSA ($\rho = 0.692$), yet VLA readout surpasses it in functional decodability ($R^2 = 0.678$ vs.\ $0.634$).
The LLM's role is thus not to enhance visual geometry but to route and integrate instruction-conditioned information at the readout position.

\paragraph{Architectural implications.}
Three design considerations follow.
First, vision encoder quality may matter more than LLM scale: the external encoder ceiling ($\rho = 0.692$) exceeds all internal representations, and training yields only modest geometric gains ($\Delta = 0.017$, $3\%$).
Second, the entry-exit attention pattern (L0--L1 and L31) suggests that intermediate layers contribute less to instruction routing, raising the possibility of layer pruning or early exit for inference efficiency.
Third, since instructions route exclusively through the readout token, architectures could explore introducing readout-like tokens earlier in the sequence rather than relying on the final position.
Linear CKA reverses the RSA ordering (Appendix~\ref{sec:metric_sensitivity_appendix}), reflecting its sensitivity to global covariance structure vs.\ RSA's rank-order focus; we argue RSA is more appropriate for cross-dimensional comparison (\S\ref{sec:rsa}), but acknowledge that geometric conclusions are metric-dependent.

\subsection{Limitations}
We study OpenVLA-7B on LIBERO tabletop tasks; generalization to other architectures (e.g., RT-2~\cite{brohan2023_rt2}, $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024_pi0}), locomotion, or dexterous manipulation remains open.
The DINOv2 comparison uses \texttt{cls\_token} while VLA image representations use mean-pooled image tokens; even under matched aggregation (\texttt{patch\_mean}: $\rho = 0.631$ vs.\ VLA peak $0.628$), DINOv2 remains comparable, supporting the qualitative conclusion.
MLP probes may underestimate representational capacity, and our post-hoc analyses do not address whether architectural interventions would preserve action quality in closed-loop evaluation.

